<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(h) </span>Activation of Experts</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf9" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.08486v1/expert_gamma_heatmap_clean.png" id="S5.F4.sf9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/118;" width="476" height="118" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(i) </span>Activation of Experts via Inc. Data</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Ablation study on LLaMA-2-7B. Graphs (a–g) show the impact of various components—finetuning steps, gate loss, regularization loss, temperature-scaled softmax, entropy regularization, KL divergence, and expert selection—on alignment task performance (WR, SS, TI). Graphs (h) and (i) visualize expert activation patterns across datasets and alignment axes, revealing how incoming data types differentially engage experts. Inc refers to Incoming in graph (i).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Impact of Mixture of Calibrated Experts (MoCaE):</span>
 Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.1 Comparison to State-of-the-Art ‣ 5 Experimental Results and Analysis ‣ Too Helpful, Too Harmless, Too Honest or Just Right?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> compares our proposed MoCaE setup with H<sup id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>Fusion across various configurations. Our approach consistently outperforms the baseline, demonstrating that modular expert design and dynamic calibration lead to more robust alignment. The core advantage stems from TrinityX’s ability to aggregate specialized knowledge through calibrated expert routing. By selectively invoking honesty-, helpfulness-, and harmlessness-aligned experts, the model generates more context-aware and alignment-consistent outputs. This results in a WR (w/ LLaMA-2-7B) improvement from 72.00% to 93.33% and a 21.68%-point gain in Avg score in the base MoCaE setting. Adding Gating Loss (GL) improves expert selection by penalizing indecisive gating behavior, while Regularization Loss (RL) mitigates overfitting and encourages diverse expert activation. Together, these yield further improvements in WR and TI while maintaining low SS, reflecting more precise and reliable responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the full TrinityX pipeline—combining fine-tuning, MoCaE, GL, and RL—achieves the strongest results across all metrics (w/ LLaMA-2-7B): 96.75% WR, 98.66% TI, and a peak Avg score of 55.12%. These gains confirm that TrinityX effectively integrates alignment-aware fine-tuning with modular, task-sensitive expert routing, enabling superior generalization and instruction-following capability. Notably, DeepSeek-7B consistently achieves superior performance across all metrics, particularly under TrinityX, where it yields the highest Avg: 52.09%, highest TI: 91.33%, and lowest SS: 30.88%. These results suggest a strong capacity for truthfulness with minimal overgeneration. DeepSeek-7B also demonstrates balanced and reliable performance, attaining a WR of 90.05% under TrinityX—surpassing Mistral-7B and Gemma-7B not only in raw output correctness but also in semantic precision and alignment. This underscores the efficacy of TrinityX as a general fine-tuning strategy across different model architectures.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation Analysis:</span> We conduct a detailed ablation to understand the contribution of each component in TrinityX. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.1 Comparison to State-of-the-Art ‣ 5 Experimental Results and Analysis ‣ Too Helpful, Too Harmless, Too Honest or Just Right?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, component-wise isolation reveals how optimization dynamics affect alignment metrics. Subfigure (a) shows that while WR consistently increases with fine-tuning steps, SS and TI fluctuate—highlighting tradeoffs in optimization. Introducing GL, as seen in (b), significantly boosts WR from 36.75% to 87.02% and improves TI. Similarly, (c) shows that RL further enhances SS and TI, confirming its utility in preventing overfitting and promoting diverse expert activation. Other configurations illustrate key tradeoffs: (d) shows that overly aggressive calibration can improve SS by 55.55% but reduce WR, suggesting overconfidence. In contrast, (e) and (f) demonstrate more balanced gains across metrics, particularly in TI and helpfulness, albeit with minor dips in safety. The impact of expert selection is underscored by (g), which demonstrates that the use of the top-1 expert substantially enhances WR (up to 74.6%) in comparison to SS and TI. The usage of expert activations across datasets is varied but proportionate, as illustrated in (h), suggesting that specialization is dependent on the dataset. Whereas, (i) illustrates that experts are activated differentially by various alignment axes, with helpfulness eliciting stronger activations, indicating a higher level of expert engagement.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Methods</span></th>
